{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.PhiBProd","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.NormedSpace.MultipliableUniformlyOn","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.LocallyUniformLimit"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_denProd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_denProd","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardApplicability.lean#L176-L189","doc":"The denominator half is entire, on the same terms. Together with\n`differentiable_numProd` this is the repair: Hadamard applies to each half,\nnever to `phiBProd` itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_numProd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_numProd","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardApplicability.lean#L156-L174","doc":"The numerator half of the modular-double product is entire.\n\nThe domination hypothesis is carried EXPLICITLY, matching `PhiBProd`'s own\nstated discipline: that file carries its `Multipliable` inputs as named\nhypotheses rather than discharging them, because they are the analytic\nconvergence facts (geometric bounds for `Im b² > 0`). Same input, same\ntreatment — the point here is the STRUCTURAL claim that the numerator is entire\nGIVEN convergence, which is what the ratio is not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_tprod_one_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.differentiable_tprod_one_add","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardApplicability.lean#L121-L154","doc":"**If every `f n` is entire and the family is dominated by a summable\nsequence on every ball, then `z ↦ ∏' n, (1 + f n z)` is entire.**\n\nThe general fact behind the repair. Mathlib supplies locally uniform\nmultipliability from a summable dominant\n(`Summable.hasProdLocallyUniformlyOn_nat_one_add`) and holomorphy of locally\nuniform limits (`TendstoLocallyUniformlyOn.differentiableOn`); this stitches\nthem and passes from \"on every ball\" to entire, differentiability being local. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.denFactor_eq_zero_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.denFactor_eq_zero_shift","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardApplicability.lean#L93-L112","doc":"The vanishing point is not a single accident: shifting `z` by any multiple of\n`i b` along the dual period keeps the `k = 0` factor at zero, so the obstruction\nrecurs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.denFactor_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardApplicability.denFactor_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardApplicability.lean#L75-L91","doc":"The `k = 0` denominator factor of the modular-double product vanishes at an\nexplicit point, for every `b ≠ 0`.\n\n`phiBProd` is defined as a quotient by `∏' k, denFactor b z k`, so this is the\nobstruction to `phiBProd` being entire — see the module docstring for exactly\nwhat is and is not concluded from it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}